WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.610
You've been on the show before.

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:04.730
You were here promoting Apollo 13 once.

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:05.740
Yes.
You came on the program

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.220
and I think really helped that picture out

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:08.578
with getting the word out.

00:00:08.578 --> 00:00:09.411
(audience laughing)

00:00:09.411 --> 00:00:10.920
You're key to every marketing strategy

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:11.920
in Hollywood, Conan.

00:00:14.961 --> 00:00:15.940
And you were very much involved

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.537
in the "From the Earth
to the Moon" series.

00:00:17.537 --> 00:00:19.280
"From the Earth to the
Moon," yes, for HBO.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.620
When did this passion for space begin?

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.270
Were you passionate about
this when you were young?

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.240
Well, yeah, I was 'cause I just thought it

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.110
was very, very cool and glamorous

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.570
to do things like go into outer space.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.450
And like do a space walk.

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:34.140
So much so we had a--

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.480
Now on the West Coast we had these things

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:36.640
called Doughboy pools,

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.630
you'd call them an above-ground
pool here on the East Coast,

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.030
but Doughboy made them, so
there was always like a,

00:00:42.030 --> 00:00:45.830
it's a aluminum with a liner in it,

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.100
you fill that with water,

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.220
and about three weeks later
the bugs start growin' in it,

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.700
but for three weeks you
would actually swim in it

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:52.670
like it was a swimming pool.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:57.070
So I would go out and have
my own simulated space walks

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:58.770
in the Doughboy pool.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:00:59.603
How does that work?

00:00:59.603 --> 00:01:02.870
Well, what you do you wear
baggy shorts, cut-offs,

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:06.640
and you put a brick right
in the back of your butt,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.770
so that will hold you on
the bottom of the pool,

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:13.400
but all your limbs will
then float up like this,

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:18.130
and in order to breathe
you get a garden hose,

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:19.370
and you put it over the side,

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.905
and you stick the garden
hose in your mouth

00:01:21.905 --> 00:01:22.738
and then you're wearin'--

00:01:22.738 --> 00:01:24.920
I would wear a pair of
like literally you get 'em

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.570
from like Ben Frank's
that cost like 99 cent

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.040
swimming goggles which is a hard rubber

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:33.007
with a plate of plastic on it.

00:01:33.007 --> 00:01:35.260
And so I sit there and under three feet

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.670
the pressure actually pushes
that against your face,

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:39.120
so I'd be down there with
this thing in my face

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.650
and a garden hose in my mouth,

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.092
and I would be pretending
to walk in space.

00:01:43.092 --> 00:01:44.269
(audience laughing)

00:01:44.269 --> 00:01:45.321
(applauding)

00:01:45.321 --> 00:01:46.571
It works great.

00:01:48.982 --> 00:01:51.770
You know, there's a word
for that guy, knot head.

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.290
Knot head, yeah.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:55.860
It always worked great till
it was time for dinner,

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:56.697
'cause my Mom would say,

00:01:56.697 --> 00:01:58.580
"Larry, go out and get
your brother outta the pool

00:01:58.580 --> 00:01:59.413
off his space walk.

00:01:59.413 --> 00:02:00.246
It's time for dinner."

00:02:00.246 --> 00:02:01.800
And he would go out there and essentially

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.375
get the garden hose,
put a crimp in it, so--

00:02:04.375 --> 00:02:05.840
(Conan laughing)
(audience laughing)

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.290
So I'm down there, like that.

00:02:10.058 --> 00:02:13.190
It is very hard to get
a brick outta your pants

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:14.570
when you're fighting for your life

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:18.817
on the bottom of a
three-foot swimmin' pool.

00:02:18.817 --> 00:02:19.660
That would be the worst.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.700
If God forbid something happened to you,

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.760
that'd be the most embarrassing way to go.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.800
It would be bad, but what a hilarious

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:28.103
little snippet of newspaper
you would have to put on.

00:02:28.103 --> 00:02:29.737
I would see this from the
"(indistinct) Daily News,"

00:02:29.737 --> 00:02:31.987
"Child Drowns with Brick
in Bottom of Pants."

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:33.916
I can't tell you how--

00:02:33.916 --> 00:02:35.570
That'd be a Jay Leno
clip to show on his show.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.005
Hey, look he had a brick in his pants!

00:02:38.005 --> 00:02:39.218
(audience laughing)

00:02:39.218 --> 00:02:41.635
(applauding)

00:02:43.277 --> 00:02:46.280
But I can't tell ya how many hours

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.973
I spent at the bottom
of the pool like that.

00:02:47.973 --> 00:02:50.540
I think I actually took
like comic books in there.

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:51.970
We'd just sit there and
float and read them,

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:54.730
you know, 'cause I just love the free fall

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:55.563
of the zero gravity.

00:02:55.563 --> 00:02:56.396
Sure, sure.

00:02:56.396 --> 00:02:57.970
Now were you into science fiction?

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:00.010
Did you like, for example,
did you like "Star Trek"?

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.240
Oh yeah, I'd set the clock by "Star Trek."

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.400
That was on at six o'clock on Channel 2...

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:06.943
in Oakland.

00:03:06.943 --> 00:03:11.110
And, yeah, much like when
you meet like the Beatles

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:15.270
when you get famous you
meet like the baseball team.

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:16.320
You sorta collect the moments

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:18.410
where you meet all the people, yeah.

00:03:18.410 --> 00:03:20.370
It's a big deal and I've met,

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:23.940
I saw Jimmie Doohan who played Scottie.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:24.880
Thank you very much.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:29.070
I saw him in a play in
San Francisco in 1975.

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:29.977
He was in a play called,

00:03:29.977 --> 00:03:32.170
"The Trial of James MacNeill Whistler."

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:33.277
We're actually in the audience like,

00:03:33.277 --> 00:03:35.800
"You think he has a tri-corner
in his dressing room?"

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:36.633
(audience laughing)

00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:37.466
He's an actor up there.

00:03:37.466 --> 00:03:40.640
When I met Leonard Nimoy and
I've worked with Bill Shatner

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:42.170
for some Shakespeare benefits.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.160
You know, Bill Shatner
takes some heat sometimes

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.000
from the other "Star Trek"
or from the Trekkies.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.780
Sometimes they think
it's all gone to his head

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.320
or that he's difficult.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.933
Dude, he played James Kirk.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:55.360
He was the Captain of
the Star Ship Enterprise.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:58.648
(audience cheering)
All right?

00:03:58.648 --> 00:04:03.648
I mean, I didn't see
Sulu down on that planet

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:06.869
makin' a cannon outta bamboo
to kill that dinosaur guy.

00:04:06.869 --> 00:04:08.380
(Conan laughing)
(audience laughing)

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:10.048
That was James Kirk.

00:04:10.048 --> 00:04:12.770
You didn't see like Chekov outwitting

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:14.400
the Gamesters of Triskelion.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.400
That was James Tiberius
Kirk who was, in fact,

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.690
the narrator of the whole show.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:22.657
You're right, you're right, I--

00:04:22.657 --> 00:04:24.940
"These are the voyages of
the Starship Enterprise."

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:26.142
I stand corrected.

00:04:26.142 --> 00:04:28.927
Can you imagine if Bones
McCoy had narrated that show?

00:04:28.927 --> 00:04:31.055
"I'm just a simple country doctor."

00:04:31.055 --> 00:04:32.960
(Conan laughing)
(audience laughing)

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:33.793
I didn't know what the heck was going on.

00:04:33.793 --> 00:04:35.490
"Here I am with my lifeforms

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.520
and my little whirling salt shaker

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.200
that tells me what's wrong with people."

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:40.780
Now did you ever meet Sulu?

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.234
Did you meet Sulu?

00:04:42.234 --> 00:04:43.887
I don't think I've ever met George--

00:04:43.887 --> 00:04:45.330
[Conan] George Takei?
[Tom] George Takei.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:46.650
I met George Takei.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:47.760
I talked to him on the phone.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.466
Wouldn't you love to have his pipes?

00:04:49.466 --> 00:04:50.890
I called him up because--

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.400
He had a voice way down here.
"We're on course, Captain."

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:54.990
He's got this voice right here.

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.197
He talks like that.

00:04:56.197 --> 00:04:58.050
And I called him up because
we wanted him to do a voice

00:04:58.050 --> 00:04:59.770
on "The Simpsons," and I called him up,

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:01.990
and he just was like,
"No, I'm not doing it.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:03.440
It makes fun of monorails

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.740
and I'm in charge of
transportation in San Francisco,

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.080
so screw yourself."

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.075
And then hung up.

00:05:08.075 --> 00:05:09.070
(audience laughing)

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.890
I couldn't believe it.

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:11.843
What the hell was that all about?

00:05:11.843 --> 00:05:14.150
What you're such a big
shot everybody's gonna drop

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:15.820
what they're doin' and
come do you a favor?

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:18.385
I know this was 12 years ago, too.

00:05:18.385 --> 00:05:20.090
Well, come on man the guy
oh, oh, oh, I'm sorry,

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:22.165
he got a life while you're
still in show business.

00:05:22.165 --> 00:05:23.563
(Conan laughing)

00:05:23.563 --> 00:05:24.947
What a shame, what a shame.

00:05:24.947 --> 00:05:25.780
(audience applauding)

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.980
Everything I've said so far!

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:28.813
Wrong.

00:05:30.907 --> 00:05:32.960
I have so much anger towards Sulu,

00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.890
and I wanted to work it out
using your valuable time.

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:36.740
You've got things to do.

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:38.137
International movie star, and I'm like,

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:40.110
"No, we gotta talk about
Sulu and how he screwed me!"

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.150
Listen, I've still got
something about that guy

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:44.463
with that big ear that's
saying, "Come to Eden, brother."

00:05:44.463 --> 00:05:46.771
He still gives me the willies, man.

00:05:46.771 --> 00:05:47.620
♪ Come to Eden ♪

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.520
That dude, oh, don't say it, don't say it.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:50.809
Yeah, yeah, all right.

00:05:50.809 --> 00:05:51.720
Gives me bad dreams.

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.527
Oh, so suddenly you're too
important to like that guy?

00:05:53.527 --> 00:05:54.360
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:05:55.230 --> 00:05:56.220
Turnabout is fair play.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:57.053
Exactly.

00:05:57.053 --> 00:05:58.203
Turnabout is fair play.

